window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.4","title":"z-Tests for Means and Proportions","goal":"Run a z-test for a mean (the 50 bolts) and a z-test for a proportion (184 of 200 customers satisfied), by hand and with statsmodels.","question":"A factory line has always made 10 mm bolts with a known spread of 0.5 mm. An engineer samples 50 bolts and finds a mean of 10.14 mm: has the process drifted? The same team also checks a company's claim of 95% customer satisfaction, when a survey of 200 customers finds 184 satisfied (92%). Is the true satisfaction rate really lower than claimed?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"A z-test for a mean, by hand","text":"H0: the true mean bolt diameter is 10 mm. H1: it is not 10 mm (it has drifted). Sigma = 0.5 mm is known, 50 bolts average 10.14 mm. The z statistic is the gap divided by the standard error, and the two-sided p-value comes from the normal curve.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nmu0, sigma, n, xbar = 10, 0.5, 50, 10.14\n\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nz = (xbar - mu0) / se\np = 2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z))\nci = (xbar - 1.96 * se, xbar + 1.96 * se)\n\nprint(f'z = {z:.3f}, p = {p:.4f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci[0]:.3f}, {ci[1]:.3f})')","explain":["Load NumPy for the square root, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; stats.norm gives the normal curve for the p-value.","Store the claim (10 mm), the known sigma (0.5), the sample size (50) and the sample mean (10.14) in one line.","Standard error: how much a mean of 50 bolts wobbles. It is sigma divided by the square root of n.","The z statistic: how many standard errors our mean is from the claim.","Two-sided p-value: the area beyond |z| in the upper tail, doubled to include the lower tail ('drifted either way').","A 95% confidence interval: the mean plus or minus 1.96 standard errors. It shows the plausible values of the true mean.","Print z and p, rounded, so we can compare them with the lesson's hand calculation.","Print the interval. If it excludes 10, that agrees with rejecting H0."],"meaning":"z = 1.980 and p = 0.0477. If the process were still centred on 10 mm, a sample mean this far away in either direction would turn up about 4.8% of the time. That is just below 0.05, so we reject H0, but it is borderline. The interval 10.001 to 10.279 only just excludes 10, which tells the same story.","out":"z = 1.980, p = 0.0477\n95% CI: (10.001, 10.279)","figs":[]},{"title":"The same test with ztest()","text":"statsmodels needs raw data. We build 50 pretend bolt readings with mean 10.14 and SD 0.5, so the result matches the hand calculation. Note that ztest() estimates the SD from the data rather than using a known sigma.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.weightstats import ztest\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nx = rng.normal(size=50)\nbolts = 10.14 + 0.5 * (x - x.mean()) / x.std(ddof=1)   # mean 10.14, SD 0.5\n\nz_stat, p_val = ztest(bolts, value=10, alternative='two-sided')\nprint(f'mean = {bolts.mean():.2f}, sd = {bolts.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z_stat:.3f}, p = {p_val:.4f}')","explain":["Load ztest from statsmodels, which runs the z-test on raw data instead of by hand.","Create a seeded random generator so the pretend bolts are the same every time.","Draw 50 random numbers from a standard normal curve as raw material for the pretend bolts.","Rescale them so the 50 values have exactly mean 10.14 and SD 0.5, matching the lesson. We centre, divide by their SD, then stretch.","Run the z-test on the readings against 10, two-sided. ztest returns the z statistic and the p-value.","Print the mean and SD of the pretend bolts, to confirm they match the lesson's numbers.","Print z and p so we can check that they match the by-hand answer."],"meaning":"The pretend bolts have mean 10.14 and SD 0.50, and ztest gives z = 1.980 and p = 0.0477, the same as the hand calculation. One detail: ztest estimates the SD from the data (it happens to equal 0.5 here), whereas in a real z-test the known sigma is supplied by the problem.","out":"mean = 10.14, sd = 0.50\nz = 1.980, p = 0.0477","figs":[]},{"title":"A z-test for a proportion","text":"H0: the true satisfaction rate is 0.95 (or higher). H1: it is lower than 0.95, so we test the left tail ('smaller'). We set prop_var=0.95 so the standard error uses the claimed value, as a test should.","code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportions_ztest\n\nz_prop, p_prop = proportions_ztest(count=184, nobs=200, value=0.95,\n                                   alternative='smaller', prop_var=0.95)\n\nprint(f'sample proportion = {184 / 200:.2f}')\nprint(f'z = {z_prop:.3f}')\nprint(f'left-tailed p = {p_prop:.4f}')\nprint('reject H0 at 0.05?', p_prop <= 0.05)","explain":["Load proportions_ztest, the z-test for a share of successes.","Run the test: 184 satisfied out of 200, claimed rate 0.95, alternative 'smaller' (left tail, H1: rate below 0.95).","prop_var=0.95 makes the standard error use the claimed rate, because a test assumes H0 is true.","Print the sample proportion, 184 / 200, to see how far it is from the claim.","Print the z statistic. Negative means the sample is below the claim.","Print the left-tailed p-value.","Compare p with alpha = 0.05 and print True or False for rejecting H0."],"meaning":"The sample proportion is 0.92, three points below the claim. z = -1.947 and the left-tailed p is 0.0258. If the true rate were 95%, a survey this low would happen about 2.6% of the time. That is below 0.05, so we reject H0: there is evidence the rate is below 95%. The test condition holds: 200 x 0.05 = 10.","out":"sample proportion = 0.92\nz = -1.947\nleft-tailed p = 0.0258\nreject H0 at 0.05? True","figs":[]},{"title":"Why the standard error matters","text":"If we leave out prop_var, statsmodels uses the observed 0.92 in the standard error. This is the classic mistake from the lesson, and it changes z and p.","code":"z_wrong, p_wrong = proportions_ztest(count=184, nobs=200, value=0.95,\n                                     alternative='smaller')\n\nprint(f'with the observed proportion: z = {z_wrong:.3f}, p = {p_wrong:.4f}')\nprint(f'with the claimed 0.95:         z = {z_prop:.3f}, p = {p_prop:.4f}')","explain":["Run the same test but leave out prop_var, so statsmodels uses the observed proportion 0.92 in the standard error.","The same call continues with alternative='smaller'; only the standard error differs from the previous step.","Print the result that wrongly uses the observed proportion.","Print the correct result, using the claimed 0.95, side by side for comparison."],"meaning":"Using the observed 0.92 in the standard error gives z = -1.564 and p = 0.0589, which would NOT be significant. Using the claimed 0.95, as a test should, gives z = -1.947 and p = 0.0258, which is. The decision flips on a detail. The test must ask 'what if H0 were true?', so the standard error uses p0.","out":"with the observed proportion: z = -1.564, p = 0.0589\nwith the claimed 0.95:         z = -1.947, p = 0.0258","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"# z-test for a mean (sigma known), by hand: base R has no z.test\nz <- (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50))\nz\n2 * pnorm(-abs(z))\n\n# z-test for a proportion, no continuity correction\nres <- prop.test(184, 200, p = 0.95, alternative = \"less\", correct = FALSE)\nres\n-sqrt(res$statistic)   # the z value (z squared is X-squared)","explain":["A comment (R ignores it). Base R has no z-test for a mean, so we compute it by hand.","Compute z = (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50)): the gap in standard errors.","Typing z alone prints its value.","Two-tailed p-value: twice the area below -|z| on the normal curve.","A comment: the next test is for a proportion.","prop.test with p = 0.95 and alternative = 'less' is the left-tailed proportion test. correct = FALSE turns off the continuity correction so it matches the z-test.","Print the whole result: X-squared, df, p-value and the sample estimate.","prop.test reports X-squared, which is z squared; the negative square root gives z (negative because we are below the claim)."],"expect":"z = 1.9799, two-sided p = 0.04771; X-squared = 3.7895, df = 1, p-value = 0.02579 (z = -1.9467)","read":"First z prints 1.9799 and the two-sided p 0.04771, below 0.05 (reject H0 for the bolts). Then prop.test shows X-squared = 3.7895 and p-value = 0.02579, the left-tailed p for the satisfaction claim. The last line gives z = -1.9467.","note":"prop.test reports X-squared, which is z squared. For a one-sided test its p-value equals the z-test p-value, and correct = FALSE switches off the continuity correction so it matches."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (SPSS has no z-test menu; use the normal-curve functions)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / xbar mu0 sigma n x m p0.\nBEGIN DATA\n10.14 10 0.5 50 184 200 0.95\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z_mean = (xbar - mu0) / (sigma / SQRT(n)).\nCOMPUTE p_mean = 2 * (1 - CDF.NORMAL(ABS(z_mean), 0, 1)).\nCOMPUTE z_prop = (x / m - p0) / SQRT(p0 * (1 - p0) / m).\nCOMPUTE p_prop = CDF.NORMAL(z_prop, 0, 1).\nLIST z_mean p_mean z_prop p_prop.","explain":["Define seven values: bolt mean, claimed mean, sigma, n, satisfied customers (x), surveyed (m) and claimed rate p0.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","One data row with the seven values in the same order as the variable names.","Marks the end of the data.","z for the bolts: the gap divided by sigma over the square root of n.","Two-tailed p for the bolts, from the area beyond |z| on the normal curve.","z for the proportion: sample share minus claimed share, divided by the standard error using p0.","Left-tailed p for the proportion: the area to the left of z.","LIST prints the four results so we can read them."],"read":"LIST shows z_mean 1.98 and p_mean 0.05 (display rounds 0.0477), then z_prop -1.95 and p_prop 0.03 (0.0258). Widen the columns or double-click the table to see more decimals. Both p-values are below 0.05, so both nulls are rejected.","note":"The first test uses the known sigma; the second is the left-tailed proportion test using p0 in the standard error. LIST shows 2 decimals by default; widen the columns in the output table to see more."},"stata":{"code":"display (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50))\ndisplay 2 * (1 - normal(1.9799))\nprtesti 200 184 0.95, count","explain":["display works as a calculator: z = (10.14 - 10) / (0.5 / sqrt(50)) = 1.9799.","Two-tailed p: normal() is the area to the left, so 2 x (1 - normal(z)) doubles the upper tail.","prtesti is the one-proportion z-test from summary numbers: 200 customers, 184 satisfied (count), claimed rate 0.95."],"read":"The first line shows 1.9799 and the second .04771. In the prtesti output, find z = -1.9467 and the left-tailed line Pr(Z < z) = 0.0258. Both are below 0.05, so both nulls are rejected.","note":"Stata has no one-line z-test for a mean from summary numbers in older versions, so the first two lines work by hand. prtesti is the one-proportion z-test (it uses 0.95 in the standard error); read the Pr(Z < z) line for the left-tailed p-value."},"conclusion":"Both claims fail at the 5% level, but only just. The bolt mean of 10.14 mm is significantly different from 10 mm (p = 0.0477), and the satisfaction rate of 92% is significantly below 95% (p = 0.0258). Caution: with p-values this close to 0.05, and an interval that barely excludes 10, treat these as warnings to check the line and collect more data, not as firm proof.","practice":{"scenario":"A hospital says that at most 10% of its patients are readmitted within 30 days. An auditor reviews 150 patient records and finds that 21 were readmitted. Is there evidence that the readmission rate is HIGHER than 10%? Use a one-sample z-test for a proportion, with the claimed rate in the standard error.","data":"readmitted, patients, p0 = 21, 150, 0.10   # readmitted patients, patients audited, claimed rate","tasks":["Find the sample proportion and check n x p0 and n x (1 - p0) are at least 10.","Run the right-tailed z-test (alternative='larger') with prop_var=p0 and report z and p.","Run the two-sided version and compare the p-values.","Decide at alpha = 0.05 and say what the result does not show."],"hint":"Use proportions_ztest(count, nobs, value=p0, alternative='larger', prop_var=p0). The two-sided p is double the one-sided p for a symmetric statistic.","solution":{"code":"from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportions_ztest\n\nreadmitted, patients, p0 = 21, 150, 0.10\nprint(f'sample proportion = {readmitted / patients:.3f}')\nprint('conditions:', patients * p0, patients * (1 - p0))\nz, p = proportions_ztest(readmitted, patients, value=p0, alternative='larger', prop_var=p0)\nprint(f'z = {z:.3f}, right-tailed p = {p:.4f}')\nz2, p2 = proportions_ztest(readmitted, patients, value=p0, alternative='two-sided', prop_var=p0)\nprint(f'two-sided p = {p2:.4f}')\nprint('reject H0 at 0.05?', p <= 0.05)","answer":"The sample proportion is 0.140 and the conditions hold (15 and 135, both at least 10). z = 1.633 and the right-tailed p is 0.0512, just above 0.05, so we fail to reject H0; the two-sided p is 0.1025. The rate looks higher than 10%, but the evidence is borderline, not conclusive. It does not prove the rate is 10%. More records could settle it.","out":"sample proportion = 0.140\nconditions: 15.0 135.0\nz = 1.633, right-tailed p = 0.0512\ntwo-sided p = 0.1025\nreject H0 at 0.05? False","figs":[]}}});
